<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Package sources for this solution. <clear /> drops any inherited sources
    from machine-level NuGet.Config so the build is reproducible from this
    file alone. Directory.Build.props turns on RestorePackagesWithLockFile, and
    without this file the committed packages.lock.json claimed a
    reproducibility it could not deliver: it resolved against whatever
    machine-level configuration happened to be present, which a different
    clone need not have.

    Deliberately no `github` source and no packageSourceCredentials block.
    This repository is the producer of HalHeinrich.Numerics.BigRational, not a
    consumer of it, and it references no HalHeinrich.* package at all - so the
    GITHUB_PACKAGES_PAT the consuming members need would be a restore failure
    mode here with nothing behind it. Publishing is unaffected: the publish
    workflow names its feed inline, in the source argument of its
    `dotnet nuget push` step, so no source in this file is read on that path.
    Add the source back only if a HalHeinrich.* package is ever referenced
    from this repo.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Deliberately no packageSourceMapping. It is not omitted by oversight and
    should not be added to match the members that carry one.

    With one declared source it would be inert for every package this repo
    restores: mapping `*` to nuget.org selects exactly the resolution no
    mapping at all produces. Its only effect would arrive on the day a second
    source is added, and measured on SDK 10.0.400 that effect points the wrong
    way. With a mapping present NuGet considers only the sources whose patterns
    match a given package id and names the rest as "not considered" in the
    restore error - so a `*` mapped to nuget.org would exclude a newly added
    github feed from every package, the HalHeinrich.* one that motivated adding
    the feed included. The private name would then be sought on the public feed
    and nowhere else, which is the direction dependency confusion runs rather
    than a defence against it.

    The defence that does work cannot be written in advance, because it has to
    name the source it protects. This repo is the producer of
    HalHeinrich.Numerics.BigRational and consumes no HalHeinrich.* package, so
    there is nothing to protect here yet; if such a PackageReference is ever
    added, add the github source above and, in that same change, a
    packageSourceMapping section mapping `HalHeinrich.*` to github and `*` to
    nuget.org.
  -->

</configuration>
